Prettier breaks a CSS pseudo-class formed inside a template literal

**Prettier 2.3.2**
[Playground link](https://prettier.io/playground/#-)
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```sh
--parser babel
```

**Input:**
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```jsx
const focus = true ? 'focus-visible' : 'focus';
const styles = css`
  /* A space is inserted after the ':' which breaks the pseudoclass */
  .content:${focus} {
    border-color: red;
  }
`;
```

**Output:**
<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```jsx
const focus = true ? "focus-visible" : "focus";
const styles = css`
  /* A space is inserted after the ':' which breaks the pseudoclass */
  .content: ${focus} {
    border-color: red;
  }
`;

```

**Expected behavior:**
I expect a space to not be inserted after the `:` so that the pseudoclass is well-formed, ie the input should be unchanged.

This is a pattern used in codebases [such as FAST](https://github.com/microsoft/fast/blob/bf9780907aafcedfc8b4d57668b20e28a5ad241f/packages/web-components/fast-components/src/styles/patterns/button.ts#L86) and exported in the public API so is likely used by many component libraries built on FAST.

