Range formatting appends a stray token outside the range when the range starts with a comment in an arrow-function body

**Prettier 3.8.4**, Node 22.23.1, `parser: "babel"`, default options.

Range formatting appends a stray token **outside the requested range** when the range starts with a comment inside an arrow-function body. Two shapes, two different outcomes — one produces code that no longer parses, the other produces valid-but-wrong code.

This is the same symptom as #12964 (closed, fixed for 2.8 by #13173). **That issue's own repro no longer reproduces on 3.8.4 — I verified it — but the two shapes below do.** So this looks like variants the fix did not cover rather than a regression of the same input.

### Reproduction

```js
import * as prettier from "prettier";

// A — arrow passed as a call argument
const A = "beforeEach(() => {\n  // c\n  a();\n});\n";
console.log(await prettier.format(A, { parser: "babel", rangeStart: 19, rangeEnd: 32 }));

// B — arrow assigned to a const
const B = "const f = () => {\n  // c\n  a();\n};\n";
console.log(await prettier.format(B, { parser: "babel", rangeStart: 18, rangeEnd: 31 }));
```

In both cases the range covers the comment line plus the statement after it.

### Actual output

**A** — the closing `});` becomes `};);`, which is not parseable:

```js
beforeEach(() => {
  // c
  a();
};);
```

Re-formatting that output fails with `Unexpected token, expected "," (4:2)`.

**B** — the closing `};` becomes `};;` — an extra empty statement:

```js
const f = () => {
  // c
  a();
};;
```

This one *does* parse, which makes it the quieter of the two: a caller that validates range-format output by re-parsing it will accept the corrupted result.

### Expected output

Input unchanged in both cases — the code inside the range is already formatted, and nothing outside the range should be touched.

