CSS: custom property with only \!important\ as value is not idempotent — extra space added on every format

**Prettier 3.9.6**
[Playground link](https://prettier.io/playground/#N4Igxg9gdgLgprEAuEBDABMdBabAPJdAQgEsBbABwgCcZVYBudAXxABoQIKYToBnZKAqpqfONWTg+A5syA)

```sh
# Options (if any):
--parser css
```

**Input:**

```css
a { --x: !important; }
```

**Output (first format):**

```css
a {
  --x:  !important;
}
```

**Output (second format):**

```css
a {
  --x:   !important;
}
```

**Output (third format):**

```css
a {
  --x:    !important;
}
```

**Expected output:**

```css
a {
  --x: !important;
}
```

**Why?**

Formatting is not idempotent: every time a CSS declaration whose custom property value is only `!important` (e.g. `--x: !important`) is formatted, an extra space is inserted before `!important`. This also affects `scss` and `less` parsers, and any indentation level (also inside `@media` etc.). Since editor "format on save" re-formats on every save, the file keeps growing by one space each time — the output never stabilizes.

Root cause: postcss parses `--x: !important` with `value === " "` (the space after the colon is part of the value for custom properties) and `raws.important === "!important"`:

```js
// postcss.parse("a { --x: !important; }").first.first
{ prop: "--x", value: " ", raws: { before: " ", between: ":", important: "!important" }, important: true }
```

`printCssDeclaration` in `src/language-css/print/css-declaration.js` then prints `value` (the space) followed by `node.raws.important.replace(/\s*!\s*important/i, " !important")`, producing two spaces. On the next parse the value becomes `"  "`, then `"   "`, etc.

Note: `a { --x: red !important; }` is stable — the problem only occurs when the value is whitespace-only (i.e. `!important` is the only content). The existing test `tests/format/css/important/important.css` only covers a normal property (`max-height: none !important`), so this case is not covered.

